WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.270
JAMES MALONE: Hello everybody.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.170
I'm James Malone.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.450
I'm a product manager
on Google Cloud.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.270
And one of the products that
I am product manager for

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:11.620
is Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.850
And today I want to talk to
you about using the open source

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.120
set of tools that are in the
Apache and Spark ecosystem

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.180
at data processing on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.262
So we're going to talk
about a few things today.

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:26.470
First we're going to talk
about the tools themselves.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.630
They're great, they
bring a lot of benefits,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.064
but they also bring a lot of
complexities and headaches.

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:32.439
And we're going
to talk about how

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.740
we want to solve that
using hardware and software

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.590
innovations that are a part
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.759
I'm going to put my money where
my mouth is and do a live demo.

00:00:41.759 --> 00:00:44.050
And we're going to create a
pretty big Spark and Hadoop

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.949
cluster to show you what
we can do at scale, some

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:49.990
of the speeds, and some
of the tools that we have

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.732
and that we bring to the
Spark and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:00:52.732 --> 00:00:54.190
And then from there
I want to share

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.900
a bunch of really
new exciting things

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.877
that we just launched in
preparation for Google Next.

00:01:00.877 --> 00:01:02.710
So the first thing that
I want to talk about

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:06.100
is the complexity of the
Spark and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.020
These tools are
really, really great,

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.277
but they often bring a lot of
painful headaches with them.

00:01:13.277 --> 00:01:15.610
So typically when you set up
a Spark and Hadoop cluster,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.270
it's not a sort of
simple or linear process.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.480
If you are familiar with
setting up a Spark and Hadoop

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.270
cluster on premise
especially, there's

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.380
a lot of steps involved.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.600
You have to get your hardware
and configure that hardware.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.840
But that's only step one.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.370
Once you have the
hardware, you then

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.650
need to get the
open source bits.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.654
Sometimes you might
do this vanilla,

00:01:37.654 --> 00:01:39.820
or sometimes you might use
a Hadoop or Spark vendor,

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.070
like Cloudera or Hortonworks.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.030
But then you have to install
that software and configure it,

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.980
tune it, and only
really after you're

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.520
done with that are you actually
ready to start processing data.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.810
Now, if you wanted to use Spark
and Hadoop on day zero or day

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.850
one, you're probably not using
Spark and Hadoop on day one

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:01.660
with this process.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.330
It's probably taken
days, weeks, or months

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.319
to actually get this
working properly.

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:08.110
There's even more
headaches if you actually

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.060
want to set up multiple
clusters, maybe

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.780
cluster for production
cluster for development.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.360
So the scaling makes your
life really difficult.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.840
So say you start
off with a cluster

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.750
and you want to expand
it because you ran out

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.330
of HDFS space, which
is used to store data

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.040
and Spark and Hadoop clusters.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.370
Well, there's going to be a lag
of time between when you have

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.470
the capacity that you
start with and have

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.120
the capacity that you need.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.610
This also may require you to
actually take your resources

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.370
offline or have
impaired functionality.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.620
Probably your
business or the need

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.720
to actually process
your data is not

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.060
stopping for you
while you're actually

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.140
scaling this cluster up.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.790
You're actually probably
compounding matters,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.230
and things are
probably piling up

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.110
that you wish you could get to.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.240
You also have to really
babysit utilization.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.220
And this is a really
big problem, especially

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.960
for accountants.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.460
You're paying for
a lot of capacity,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.700
but you're probably not running
your big data infrastructure

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.110
at 100% all the time.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.827
That means that you're probably
paying for overcapacity.

00:03:05.827 --> 00:03:07.660
Conversely, you could
pay for undercapacity,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.610
but that's not really a
great solution either,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.400
because then you have less
capacity than you actually

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.690
need to answer any
of your questions.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.680
So you have to really be very
careful about how you plan out

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.830
your capacity and try and
smooth out your utilization

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.330
as much as possible.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.460
Ultimately, you're not
paying for what you use.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.380
This is a really big problem.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.260
And one of the big complaints of
the Spark and Hadoop ecosystem

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.170
are they're great
tools, and oddly enough,

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.120
a lot of it's free open source.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.325
But it can be very, very
costly to run and maintain.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.100
We don't want you to
have this problem.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.340
Spark and Hadoop
are great tools,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.440
and they bring a
lot to the table.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.020
But you shouldn't worry
about the cost and complexity

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.130
of running these tools to
take advantage of them.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.220
So I did mention
Cloudera and Hortonworks.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.920
If anybody in the room is
familiar with Cloudera,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.490
Hortonworks, or MapR and you're
using them, just to throw out,

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.490
all three are supported
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.210
They all have different
ways to bootstrap clusters,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.070
so there's things like
Cloudera Manager or Ambari

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:11.464
or Cloudbreak.

00:04:11.464 --> 00:04:13.880
If you want to use those on
Google Cloud, you totally can.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.130
And you'd get a lot of
benefits from doing that.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.480
You're going to get
low pricing for VMs,

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.680
you're going be able
to use cloud storage.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.436
You could possibly use Cloud
Bigtable instead of HBase.

00:04:24.436 --> 00:04:26.060
So there's a lot of
benefits for doing.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.796
That so just to say, if
you are tied to these

00:04:28.796 --> 00:04:30.170
or really love
these vendors, you

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.420
can definitely bring them
and lift and shift them

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.610
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.560
So we kind of offer
a suite of ways

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.110
to run the giant Apache
and Spark ecosystem.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.970
And that's maybe
actually worth a note.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.190
So Apache and Spark are
sort of the two most

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:46.190
well-known components, but
it's a really big ecosystem

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:47.404
of related tools.

00:04:47.404 --> 00:04:49.070
And I've never really
found a better way

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.590
of referring to it, other than
the Apache Spark and Hadoop

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.800
ecosystem.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.060
So there's a lot of tools that
you can run on these clusters.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.280
And we have a whole
host of options

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.310
to allow you to run this
software on Cloud platform.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:05.339
As I mentioned, you could take
your on-premise or other cloud

00:05:05.339 --> 00:05:07.130
Hadoop vendor and bring
it to Google Cloud,

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.414
like a Cloudera or Hortonworks.

00:05:09.414 --> 00:05:11.330
We started off the journey
with Cloud Dataproc

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.540
by offering what we call bdutil,
which is a set of command line

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:15.300
scripts.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.542
They're open source
and they're on GitHub,

00:05:17.542 --> 00:05:20.000
and they allow you to bootstrap
kind of a lightweight Spark

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.410
and Hadoop cluster.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.830
Some of our large customers
actually started using bdutil

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.060
and have moved to Dataproc.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.700
But if you want to use
bdutil and get started

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.040
with a kind of lightweight
Spark and Hadoop cluster,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.169
you totally can.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:34.460
But with Cloud Dataproc, we
thought we could do better.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.640
With bdutil you're still
kind of manually setting up

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:38.150
the cluster.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.730
There's still a little
babysitting you have to do.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.430
It can be kind of
difficult to submit jobs.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.680
For instance, you might
have to use a command line

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.090
to do a lot of these things.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:48.950
That might not be a
problem for some users,

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.660
but for your average
business user,

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.400
you probably don't
want them to have

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.390
to use an SSH window or
terminal window to go interact

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.230
with Spark and Hadoop clusters.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.690
So Cloud Dataproc,
we really tried

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.580
to do a little bit
better and focus

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.767
on everything except your data.

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:06.350
So we take care of
the infrastructure,

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:07.850
we take care of the software.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.579
You bring your
data and your jobs,

00:06:09.579 --> 00:06:11.870
and you can use Spark and
Hadoop without worrying about

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.620
too much else.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.130
So there's a lot of open source
components, as I mentioned.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.250
And they map to different
Google Cloud products

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.030
in different ways.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:26.850
So a lot of these products
you can run on Cloud Dataproc.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.410
So if you're familiar
with the Hadoop ecosystem,

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.010
Cloud Dataproc uses YARN.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.350
And a lot of the applications
in that ecosystem

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.346
will run on YARN.

00:06:35.346 --> 00:06:37.095
So if you're using
them, you can generally

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:39.480
come and use Cloud Dataproc.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.660
We also have other services
that are in their own ways,

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.020
tied to some of these
open source products.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.550
And I'll get to how
they're tied in a minute.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.770
But for instance, if
you're using HBase,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.960
you might want to check
out Cloud Bigtable.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.970
If you're interested in Apache
Beam or Crunch or Flume,

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.020
Cloud Dataflow is a
really great solution

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.980
that you might
want to check out.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.560
And additionally, if you're
tied to things like Impala

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.080
or Century or HDFS,
we have products

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:10.080
that map to sort of all
of these technologies.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.770
So Cloud Dataproc handles, I
would say the majority of them.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.270
But around the
edges, we also have

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.219
a lot of products that
can fill in the gaps.

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:20.010
So I mentioned that
these products were all

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.470
inspired by open source
technologies in different ways.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.220
So a lot of the open
source ecosystem

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.530
is based on papers
that Google has

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.130
released throughout the years.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:34.500
So for example, the relationship
between Bigtable and HBase.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.140
In other cases, the
open source ecosystem

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.810
has kind of evolved
in and of itself.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.524
At this point, Google's
also very actively involved

00:07:42.524 --> 00:07:44.190
in contributing back
to that open source

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.520
ecosystem, a great example
being Apache Beam, which

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.360
allows you to create batch or
stream pipelines in kind of one

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.550
language with one
SDK and one model,

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.200
and run them on many
different routers,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.470
rather it's Cloud Dataflow
or Flink or Spark.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.630
So interestingly,
a lot of the things

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.390
that run on Cloud Dataproc
were inspired by things

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.980
that Google has
done in the past.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.600
And a lot of things that could
run on Dataproc in the future

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.140
are inspired by things that
Google is doing in the present

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.050
to try and grow this
ecosystem all together.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.590
So let's talk about Cloud
Dataproc just a little bit more

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.130
specifically.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.240
So Cloud Dataproc is a way
to create Spark and Hadoop

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:25.560
clusters that are
generally fast, low cost,

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.030
and easy to use.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.880
And the way that we do that is
we rely on a lot of the things

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.270
that the Google Cloud Platform
brings to the Spark and Hadoop

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.350
ecosystem.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.330
And I'll talk about
what things we rely on,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.750
but we've both tried to focus
on making the open source

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.970
components run really well
and tuning some of them,

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.400
and I can talk
about that, but also

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.400
bring a lot of the strengths
of the Google Cloud Platform

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:48.700
to open source.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.760
And the point I make
here is, it's not

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.160
about just installing Spark
and Hadoop on some servers

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.450
and calling it good.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.490
There's a lot of really
deep integrations,

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.440
optimizations, and
thought that goes

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:04.520
into making Spark and Hadoop
fast, easy, and cost-effective.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.150
As an example, say you want
a Spark or Hadoop cluster.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.190
I started off with
that really long line

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.191
of having to rack servers
and configure servers.

00:09:12.191 --> 00:09:13.690
But even if you
don't focus on that,

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:15.680
you still have to get
the open source software

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.870
and tune it and customize
it and debug it.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.140
With Cloud Dataproc, we want
that process to be one click.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:23.570
We want you to be able to
fill out a very simple form,

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:26.100
and with one click
create your cluster.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.910
Cloud Dataproc is interesting,
and I'll show it in my demo.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:32.240
We don't abstract away all
of the complexities and knobs

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.530
in the open source ecosystem.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.770
And in some ways, Cloud Dataproc
is sort of an anti-Google Cloud

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.080
product, because we do
expose that messiness to you

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.400
if you want it.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.170
We don't necessarily
try and prevent you

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:43.100
from doing bad things.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.950
We want you to know this is
a full-on Spark and Hadoop

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:46.790
cluster.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.740
If you want to tune
specific properties

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.420
or do very specific
customizations,

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:51.740
you totally can.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.800
We try and make it easy for you
and optimize as much as we can

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.790
for you, but with a lot of these
tools, they're very knobby.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:01.670
And sometimes people want to
tune and poke those knobs.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:02.900
And we totally let you.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:04.900
So if you want, you can
configure your cluster.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:07.189
And we have a set of
tools and API endpoints

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:08.480
to try and make that very easy.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.280
So that could take 20 seconds.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.620
And by 90 seconds or so, you
should have your cluster.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:17.630
So we go from possibly
hours or days or weeks

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:21.855
to less than three
or four minutes.

00:10:21.855 --> 00:10:23.480
As I mentioned, what
we're trying to do

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.430
is bring the open
source ecosystem

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:27.110
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:28.730
So we have a lot of products.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.980
And we don't want Dataproc
to just be isolated,

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.050
because having just Spark
and Hadoop by itself

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.734
isn't super useful.

00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:36.650
We want you to be able
to use Spark and Hadoop

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:38.930
with other products that
we have in our platform,

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:41.280
and I have some of them
shown on this slide.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.030
For example, we want you to be
able to use Spark and Hadoop

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.430
with Google Cloud Storage,
which is part of the demo,

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.640
as a replacement for the
Hadoop Distributed File System.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.542
If you want to use
Bigtable, we want

00:10:51.542 --> 00:10:53.750
you to be able to use Spark
and Hadoop with Bigtable.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:55.220
Same thing goes for BigQuery.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.010
If you're using BigQuery as
an enterprise data warehouse,

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:01.340
we want you to be able to use
that powerful enterprise data

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.830
warehouse with the really
powerful Spark and Hadoop

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.050
ecosystem.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.850
We've tried to
make it very easy,

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:13.125
focusing on moving existing
workloads to Cloud Dataproc.

00:11:13.125 --> 00:11:15.500
So generally, Spark and Hadoop
have been out for a while.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.960
And in one way or another,
a lot of customers

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:21.050
have data and workloads
that they want to migrate.

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:23.630
We don't want that
migration to be painful.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:26.530
Generally that migration
should be pretty easy.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.410
You copy your data, step
one, to Google Cloud Storage.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.510
Step two, you should
be able to change

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:35.300
just a few lines of your job.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:37.400
Really, the biggest change
you should need to make

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:40.670
is just changing the URI prefix
of where your data is located.

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:44.000
Instead of being on HDFS,
it's in Cloud Storage.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.280
You really shouldn't need
to make any other changes

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:46.779
than that.

00:11:46.779 --> 00:11:48.200
So moving your
actual code should

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.150
be very, very easy,
almost as easy

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.400
as just copying your data over.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.240
And then step three, you
actually use Cloud Dataproc.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:58.040
So this is an example job.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:01.670
It's from the Spark examples.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:04.622
And it's a job that
is reading from HDFS.

00:12:04.622 --> 00:12:06.830
In this case, you can see,
and it may be hard to see,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:08.240
and I apologize.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.610
HDFS is just stricken
and we replace

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:14.330
the URI prefixed with GS
for Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:17.690
So if I wanted to move this
example job to Dataproc,

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.240
that's the only change I need
to make, aside from making sure

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.210
that the data I'm reading
exists in Cloud Storage

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.130
and has the same file structure.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:26.990
So again, very,
very easy to move

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:30.691
work, because moving work and
recoating things is not a value

00:12:30.691 --> 00:12:31.190
add.

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:35.634
You're not focused on your data,
and we don't really want that.

00:12:35.634 --> 00:12:36.800
Under the hood a little bit.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:40.550
So Cloud Dataproc is, for
all intents and purposes,

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.720
a Spark and Hadoop cluster.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.780
And you can create multiple
clusters in a project

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.150
and submit work
to those clusters.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.300
Those clusters are built
on existing Cloud Platform

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:51.000
technologies.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.880
So it's built on Compute Engine.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:58.040
Cloud Dataproc clusters have
one or more master nodes.

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.380
You have a set of worker nodes.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.630
And then you can create
preemptible worker nodes

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.390
that take advantage
of preemptible VMs.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:07.680
For anybody that's not
familiar with preemtible VMs,

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.460
they're Compute Engine VMs,
they're a special type.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:11.390
There's a tradeoff.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:13.520
They have substantially
lower cost,

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.335
but they also have a maximum
lifetime of 24 hours.

00:13:16.335 --> 00:13:18.710
They're basically built on
spare capacity and Google data

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:19.340
centers.

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.110
And if we need
that capacity back,

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:22.945
you lose the preemptible VM.

00:13:22.945 --> 00:13:24.920
Well, preemtible
VMs may be great

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:27.440
for compute-intensive workloads
with Spark and Hadoop.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.120
You can use them
with Cloud Dataproc.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.394
So again, we're trying
to not only just rely

00:13:31.394 --> 00:13:33.560
on the lower general costs
of Google Cloud platform,

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:37.390
but allow you to use the super
low cost aspects of Google

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.890
Cloud Platform to bring your
data processing costs down even

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:41.330
further.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.120
Clusters in Cloud Dataproc do
have HDFS attached to them,

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:45.860
based on persistent disk.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:47.900
We generally advise
customers don't actually

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:51.200
store any type of persistent
data on this, because A,

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.730
you don't want your
persistent data

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:55.020
to be ephemeral with your
ephemeral Spark or Hadoop

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:55.860
cluster.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.610
And then B, you can actually
get better performance often

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.470
with Google Cloud
Storage than you can

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:03.480
with HDFS, that's based
on persistent disk.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.961
And then with your clusters,
you can read and write.

00:14:05.961 --> 00:14:08.210
Generally most customers
will use Google Cloud Storage

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:10.760
to do that.

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.725
Going into a little
bit more detail,

00:14:12.725 --> 00:14:15.350
Cloud Dataproc has
an API, a REST API.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:18.590
And our client tools
talk to that REST API.

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:21.640
That's the same REST API
that anybody can use.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:23.120
And the engineering
team, which I

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.910
have to give enormous credit to,
they're a very passionate group

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.340
of people and I would not
be able to actually talk

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:31.676
about Cloud Dataproc if they
weren't dedicated and awesome,

00:14:31.676 --> 00:14:33.050
has put a lot of
time and thought

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:36.980
into making the API
expressive, yet easy to use.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:39.560
That API really has
three endpoints-- one

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.530
for managing clusters,
one for managing jobs.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.560
And there's an API
endpoint for operations.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:47.060
So on all of these
compute nodes,

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.876
we have a Cloud
Dataproc agent which

00:14:48.876 --> 00:14:50.000
talks to our control plane.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.830
And this is really
how we kind of

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:54.020
create the glue of the
Spark and Hadoop cluster

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:56.780
and allow for some
interesting things,

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:58.460
like being able to
submit and manage

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.800
work and interact with
YARN on the cluster.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.382
We build an image,
it's based on Debian.

00:15:04.382 --> 00:15:05.910
And the open source
components are

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:09.060
built using another Apache
project called Apache Bigtop.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.030
So basically we build
our own custom sort

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:15.600
of open source distribution of
the Spark and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.880
If you haven't used
Dataproc before,

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.650
the team takes great
pride in making sure

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.960
that we not only version all
of the versions of Dataprocs,

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.290
you can actually select
which bundle of components

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:27.360
you want to use and
their version set.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.000
We also generally try
to release frequently.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.180
So for example, our preview
image for Cloud Dataproc

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:39.090
is based on Hadoop 2.8,
which is late and breaking.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:42.840
Our production image right
now uses very recent versions

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.934
of both Hadoop and Spark.

00:15:44.934 --> 00:15:46.350
If you want to use
older versions,

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.510
you can always go back
and use older versions

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:49.770
of Cloud Dataproc.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:53.160
The goal here is to allow people
to use the set of versions

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:57.360
that they really want to and
not lag for weeks, months,

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.850
or quarters behind,
but also not just

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:02.640
put beta software on clusters
and force people to dog food.

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:08.200
So as I mentioned, all
of our client tools

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:09.670
are built on a REST API.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:11.920
Generally there's three
client tools, or three ways

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:13.800
that people interact
with clusters.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.420
There is the Google
Cloud Console.

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:20.920
There's the Google Cloud SDK,
which is often seen or known

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:22.420
as G Cloud.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:23.620
And then there's SSH.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.510
Since this is built
on Compute Engines,

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:27.160
you can interact with
the compute nodes

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.500
as if they were just regular
VMs, because they are.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:34.260
So you can SSH into the
master and worker nodes.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:37.360
Pricing, a very common
question for Cloud Dataproc.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:40.572
So with your--
excuse me, I should

00:16:40.572 --> 00:16:42.280
be able to pronounce
my own product name.

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:43.960
With your Cloud
Dataproc cluster,

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:46.120
you pay for the compute
and storage charges

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.720
that make up your cluster.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:51.070
And then there's a very low
$0.01 premium per virtual CPU

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:53.741
on top of that, that we charge
for all of the awesome things

00:16:53.741 --> 00:16:55.490
that we provide as
part of Cloud Dataproc.

00:16:57.977 --> 00:17:00.310
It sounds scary, but we're
actually very cost-effective,

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:04.300
if you compare Cloud
Dataproc to other providers.

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:06.099
The source was actually
moved in the slide,

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:08.109
and I kind of have to
duck here, but it's there.

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:10.480
It's actually based on
an O'Reilly article,

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.560
it's a comparison between
Amazon EMR and Cloud Dataproc.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:19.300
And you can see in
general, Cloud Dataproc

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.381
runs jobs faster.

00:17:22.381 --> 00:17:24.339
That's important because
we bill by the minute.

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:25.922
We don't round up
to the nearest hour.

00:17:25.922 --> 00:17:27.819
So if you use a
cluster for 13 minutes,

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:30.230
you pay for 13
minutes, not one hour.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:36.370
We've also really been
optimizing Cloud Dataproc

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:38.339
to make processing faster.

00:17:38.339 --> 00:17:39.880
And this is a really
important point,

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.910
because the goal is not to
just let things stagnate.

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:46.150
We actually want to speed up
your data processing workloads.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.550
It does mean that you might
use less Cloud Dataproc,

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.650
and we actually see
that as a victory.

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.930
So this is an example
of a feature which

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.060
we'll be rolling out soon
to Cloud Dataproc, where

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:58.690
we changed out the SSL mechanism
or provider used in our Cloud

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:03.040
Storage Connector from the
default implementation to one

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.450
based on Google's
fork of OpenSSL.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:08.650
And you can see that
reads have gotten,

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.750
or will get in most cases,
substantially faster.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.200
Writes will also see
an improvement as well.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:17.110
And I mention this
to really showcase,

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:20.080
a goal is to have you use
less Dataproc because you're

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:25.720
able to do more with the
same amount, or with less.

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.102
So the supported
components-- a big ecosystem,

00:18:28.102 --> 00:18:29.560
there's a lot of
stuff that you can

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.780
run on Cloud Dataproc clusters.

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:35.020
By default, we install
the most common packages.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:38.505
So that's Spark,
Hadoop, Hive, Pig.

00:18:38.505 --> 00:18:40.630
Some of those packages are
tricky because something

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:42.336
like Hadoop includes--

00:18:42.336 --> 00:18:43.960
I don't know how many
weird [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.050
subprojects and
tangents of projects.

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:49.750
But generally those are
the four core components.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:52.840
We build a lot of the
ecosystem, however, in Bigtop.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:56.480
We have a repository
with each release.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:00.010
So if you wanted to install
something like Zeppelin,

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:01.080
that's built in Bigtop.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.080
It's actually in Bigtop.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:05.800
So if you went onto a cluster or
used an initialization action,

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.600
which I will show
you in my demo,

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:10.270
you could install Zeppelin,
because we actually

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:11.279
have pre-built it.

00:19:11.279 --> 00:19:13.570
The reason I call this out,
it's a very common question

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.455
of how much can we
install on a cluster?

00:19:15.455 --> 00:19:17.836
Well, you can install
really, whatever you want.

00:19:17.836 --> 00:19:19.210
We try to keep it
lean because we

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:22.782
don't want to burden clusters
and install stuff that people

00:19:22.782 --> 00:19:25.240
aren't going to use, and is
just going to take up resources

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:28.120
and make their data processing
slower and make them sad,

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.516
and then that makes us sad.

00:19:30.516 --> 00:19:32.650
So I'm going to go to
a Cloud Dataproc demo

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:34.510
to tie together
some of the things

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:37.806
that I've mentioned thus far.

00:19:37.806 --> 00:19:39.680
In this demo, we're
going to do a few things.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.780
We are going to
create a cluster.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.141
And I'm going to create
a fairly sizable cluster.

00:19:44.141 --> 00:19:45.890
It's not the biggest
cluster in the world,

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:48.490
but I think it's modestly big.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.527
We're going to query
a large set of data.

00:19:50.527 --> 00:19:52.360
And I'll talk about the
data and the queries

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:53.800
that we're going to run.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:56.140
We're going to see the
output of those queries,

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:59.080
and that actually hints at some
of the really fancy tooling

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:00.391
around Cloud Dataproc.

00:20:00.391 --> 00:20:02.890
And then we're going to delete
the cluster, just to show you

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:05.359
that you only need the
cluster that's really designed

00:20:05.359 --> 00:20:06.400
as an ephemeral solution.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.441
You only need the cluster
as long as you actually

00:20:08.441 --> 00:20:10.950
have data to process.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:13.890
So the data that we
are going to query

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:16.530
is based on the New
York taxi dataset.

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:21.750
So from 2009 to 2015, the New
York City Limousine and Taxi

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:25.380
Commission released all
taxi trips and Uber trips

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:26.730
in New York.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:29.820
Uncompressed, the dataset
is about 270 gigabytes.

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:33.660
It's in CSV format, and
there's about 1.2 billion trips

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:35.670
in this dataset.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.100
So I'm going to switch here.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:39.720
So this is the
Google Cloud console,

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:41.430
if you have not seen it before.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:45.480
And this is the form or the
page to create a Cloud Dataproc

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:46.440
cluster.

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.320
I could create this cluster
through just a raw API call.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:50.541
I could also use G Cloud.

00:20:50.541 --> 00:20:52.290
I'm showing you through
the Cloud console,

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:55.500
just because it is generally
much easier to demo

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.607
than trying to
type in a console.

00:20:57.607 --> 00:21:00.190
First thing that we're going to
do is give the cluster a name.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:03.540
I'm going to call my
cluster james demo.

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:06.180
The second thing that you
generally will want to fill in

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:09.340
is the zone where you're
going to create a cluster.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:11.790
Cloud Dataproc is
generally available

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:14.010
wherever Compute
Engine is available.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.310
That is going to be true
as Compute Engine is

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:21.510
made available in more regions
as Google Cloud expands.

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.540
The Cloud Dataproc team wants
to be available everywhere

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.019
that Google Cloud is available.

00:21:26.019 --> 00:21:27.810
So right now, the list
is here and the list

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.120
will grow over time.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:31.650
I'm going to choose
US Central, just

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.390
because I suspect
that's going to give us

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:36.330
pretty good performance since
we're in the US right now.

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:39.480
You then choose how you want
to configure your master

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:40.350
and worker nodes.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:44.040
And these are very analogous
to traditional Hadoop master

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.780
and worker nodes.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:48.900
From master nodes, we offer
three different varieties.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:50.650
And one of them is
actually a new feature,

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:53.410
which is having a cluster
that's just one node.

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:55.560
That means your master
is your worker--

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.050
good for development,
exploration, possibly

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.940
education, lightweight
data science.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:02.940
The default option is
just having one master.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:06.150
We also offer the ability to
have high availability, which

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:07.080
is three masters.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:10.050
And that provides,
it's based on ZooKeeper

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.700
and it provides YARN and
HDFS high availability.

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.700
I'm just going to create
a standard cluster here

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:16.770
because we don't need
high availability.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.436
You can choose the
machine type that you

00:22:18.436 --> 00:22:19.610
want to use in your cluster.

00:22:19.610 --> 00:22:23.880
In the web UI here, we have all
of the standard non-shared CPU

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.120
machine types.

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:27.204
You can also use custom
VMs with Cloud Dataproc.

00:22:27.204 --> 00:22:29.370
So if you actually wanted
to use a machine type that

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:34.620
was based on six CPUs and
58.62 gigabytes of RAM,

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:38.070
you could absolutely do that
through the API and the command

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:38.830
line.

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:40.413
In this case, I'm
just going to choose

00:22:40.413 --> 00:22:43.640
an n1-standard-16
for my master node.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:45.470
And worker nodes, same thing.

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:48.650
Something that is
worth calling out here

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:52.070
is in the very near
future, Cloud Dataproc

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:55.700
will be supporting
64 core VMs, which

00:22:55.700 --> 00:23:00.060
were recently announced on
beta on Google Cloud Platform.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.636
And I called that
out because often,

00:23:01.636 --> 00:23:04.010
a lot of the improvements that
are made to the underlying

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:05.760
services of a cloud
platform, whether it's

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:08.570
Compute Engine or Cloud Storage
or our networking, Cloud

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:10.140
Dataproc benefits from those.

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:12.950
So as a lot of other components,
can Google Cloud get better,

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:14.660
it means that using
Spark and Hadoop

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:17.420
gets incrementally
better as well.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:19.940
For my worker nodes, I'm
going to use an 8 core

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:22.220
and n1-standard-8,
just in our testing.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.295
These work usually pretty well.

00:23:24.295 --> 00:23:26.420
I'm going to go ahead and
create kind of a modestly

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:27.380
large cluster here.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.530
Let's go ahead
and add 200 nodes.

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:33.380
That will give us 1600 YARN
cores, and about 4.7 terabytes

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:35.439
of YARN RAM.

00:23:35.439 --> 00:23:36.980
We're not going to
use a lot of HDFS,

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.526
so I'm actually going to lower
the amount of persistent disks

00:23:39.526 --> 00:23:41.150
that we attach because
we're not really

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:44.420
going to be writing to HDFS.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:46.730
And then you can see there's
some advanced options that

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:48.650
are optional, that
you can configure.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:51.050
I could do things
like use networking,

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:54.020
and Cloud Dataproc
supports a wide variety

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:55.807
of networking options.

00:23:55.807 --> 00:23:57.890
You can select, as I
mentioned, different versions

00:23:57.890 --> 00:23:59.600
of Cloud Dataproc.

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:01.700
In this case, the configuration
I'm going to apply

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.540
is the one [INAUDIBLE] I'm
going to install Presto

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:07.580
in my cluster, just
to show you that you

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.980
can install a wide variety
of things on Cloud Dataproc.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:13.310
And Presto is actually not part
of the sort of Apache big data

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.890
ecosystem, but I'm going
to install it and show you

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:16.492
that it works.

00:24:16.492 --> 00:24:18.200
And we're going to
run all of our queries

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:19.940
through Presto, actually.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.260
So we'll go ahead and
create that cluster.

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:24.130
All right.

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:28.570
So the cluster is in the
process of bootstrapping itself.

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:33.720
Let's go ahead and fill out
the form to submit our job.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:35.539
Now, with Cloud Dataproc,
one of the things

00:24:35.539 --> 00:24:37.080
that we've paid a
lot of attention to

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:40.840
is trying to make it really easy
to interact with your cluster.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:43.110
And often when you
want to submit work--

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:45.780
in this case, we call
them jobs-- to clusters,

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:47.580
you have to use of
complicated tooling

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:51.000
or you have to log in
and open an SSH window.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.500
And that's just no good.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.170
If you have a lot
of work to do, we

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:57.720
want it to be very
easy for you to submit

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:02.095
your Spark, or PySpark, or Spark
SQL scripts to Cloud Dataproc.

00:25:02.095 --> 00:25:03.720
The other thing that
I'll show you here

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:06.720
is the Jobs API and
the tools on top of it

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:08.670
allow us to not
only submit work,

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.680
but we can actually
see the streamed output

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:12.001
from those jobs.

00:25:12.001 --> 00:25:14.250
And the nice thing is, I
could actually submit the job

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.932
and close my computer
and throw it in a fire,

00:25:16.932 --> 00:25:18.390
and that job is
still going to run.

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.652
So it's not tied to the
persistence of that one

00:25:20.652 --> 00:25:22.860
computer, which is actually
a really annoying problem

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:24.300
if you use Spark
and Hadoop a lot,

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:25.890
that you open up
a terminal window

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.740
and if that terminal window
goes away, unless you thought

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:31.260
about it, that job is
going to just vanish

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:34.402
when you leave your computer.

00:25:34.402 --> 00:25:36.360
So first thing, I'm going
to choose the cluster

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.030
that I want to run the job on.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:41.700
The next thing I'm going to
do is choose the job type.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:45.090
Right now we support
six job types.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.340
I'm going to run Pig,
and people always

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:49.965
give me a hard time about this,
but there's a very good reason.

00:25:49.965 --> 00:25:51.798
The reason I'm going
to use Pig is because I

00:25:51.798 --> 00:25:52.920
am going to abuse Pig.

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:54.390
I'm actually just
going to use Pig

00:25:54.390 --> 00:25:56.700
to shell out to call Presto.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:59.280
A little convoluted, but
it's convoluted on purpose

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:01.230
to show you that
we're not putting up

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:02.700
bumpers or guardrails.

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:05.070
You can do really,
whatever you want.

00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:09.180
The goal here is to allow you
to have life be very easy.

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:13.900
So for example, if I wanted
to just run Spark SQL,

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:17.570
I could enter a file
with my Spark SQL

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:21.520
on Cloud Storage, or just the
raw query text, very easy.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:24.370
And me using Pig to
shell out to Presto,

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:27.282
that's a bit more
complicated and interesting.

00:26:27.282 --> 00:26:28.990
But that's really to
show you that if you

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:30.955
want to develop with
Spark and Hadoop,

00:26:30.955 --> 00:26:32.830
we're not going to try
and artificially limit

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:34.430
you and constrain you too much.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:40.950
So with my Pig script
here, I am going

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:45.120
to specify my query file, which
is inside of a Cloud Storage

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:46.130
bucket.

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:49.410
And this SQL script is going
to essentially just query

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.660
Presto with a few queries.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:55.000
Let's go take a look at the
cluster that we created.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:57.420
I was talking a little
bit, unfortunately,

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:58.540
and just blabbering a lot.

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.150
My cluster actually was ready
probably two minutes ago.

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:06.140
So our 200 node cluster with
1600 cores is ready to go.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:10.750
All of the VMs are up, and
it's ready to take work.

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:13.400
If I wanted to, I
can edit this cluster

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:16.430
and do things like add
and remove worker nodes.

00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:18.754
I can add and remove
preemptible nodes.

00:27:18.754 --> 00:27:20.420
And that's while the
cluster is running.

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:22.420
So if I wanted to add
more notes to this cluster

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:24.340
while it was running,
I totally can.

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:25.840
And just because
it sounds like fun,

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:26.964
let's go ahead and do that.

00:27:26.964 --> 00:27:28.180
So let's submit our job.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:33.022
And the job is now
running on my cluster.

00:27:33.022 --> 00:27:34.480
And as the job gets
started, you'll

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.210
actually see the job
output start spooling here

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.260
live to the screen.

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:41.020
So you can actually see
that first thing that's

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:43.270
going to happen if you
have really good eyesight

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.640
is we're going to
create a external table

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.370
and then start running queries
against that external table.

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:50.830
This is an example of us trying
to make life easy for you.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:52.975
So the job was sent
to the cluster.

00:27:52.975 --> 00:27:54.850
We're interacting with
open source components

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:57.250
and feeding the
output back to you.

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:59.357
Job's running, let's go
ahead and say oh, I'm

00:27:59.357 --> 00:28:00.940
worried about this
job, let's go ahead

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:03.550
and add 20 more nodes
or 10 more nodes,

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:07.300
just because we want
a really big cluster.

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:09.570
So we're going to go ahead
and update the cluster

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:11.320
while the job is running.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:14.260
It's not going to
impact the job at all.

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:17.640
And you can see that the job
is starting to spool output

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:20.250
to this screen live.

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.450
It's going to update.

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:23.970
You'd have the same experience
if you used the G Cloud

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:24.840
command.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.556
So if you said G Cloud,
Dataproc job submit Pig,

00:28:28.556 --> 00:28:30.180
and then fed it the
required arguments,

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:34.110
you're going to see this
output to the screen as well.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:36.740
This job is not writing
anything anywhere.

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:40.680
It's really just emitting
the results in standard out.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.570
Obviously could
save results back

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:46.770
to BigQuery or Cloud Storage
or a number of other cloud

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:49.380
products.

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:52.560
You can see that while
the cluster is resizing,

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:53.750
it doesn't go down.

00:28:53.750 --> 00:28:56.010
There is no
interruption of service.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:59.220
It just continues to
work without hassle,

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:02.471
without you needing to do
anything much more specific

00:29:02.471 --> 00:29:02.970
than that.

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:08.940
We just went and queried
all of that data.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:10.990
It took a minute and 33 seconds.

00:29:10.990 --> 00:29:12.990
What's really impressive
about that is this data

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:14.040
is in Cloud Storage.

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:17.700
So between Cloud Storage, where
this giant dataset is stored

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:19.580
and the Spark and
Hadoop cluster,

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:22.650
it was able to read
the data and query it

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:24.150
in one minute and 33 seconds.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:27.629
And that's really because
there's really fast networking

00:29:27.629 --> 00:29:28.920
in our data centers in general.

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:31.230
And Cloud Dataproc
cluster is able to use

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:32.910
that really fast
networking to go query

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:36.810
Cloud Storage in a jiffy.

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:38.850
So actually we're just
done with this cluster.

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:40.980
That's really what
I wanted to show.

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:42.720
So let's go ahead
and say, we're done.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:45.040
We actually don't need
this cluster anymore.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:46.920
We'll just go ahead and
delete that cluster.

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:48.420
And the cluster is
going to go away.

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.270
No fuss, no hassle.

00:29:51.270 --> 00:29:53.130
So what happened?

00:29:53.130 --> 00:29:56.530
We created a cluster
that was 1600 YARN cores.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:58.830
It ran the Spark and
Hadoop ecosystem.

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:01.560
In this case, we took
advantage of Presto

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:03.720
and we did it in one click.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:06.730
To put a little bit more
detail in what we did,

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:08.250
we created an
external table that

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:12.160
was based on the data in
the Cloud Storage bucket.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.920
And then we just ran
a few simple queries

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.002
against that data.

00:30:15.002 --> 00:30:16.710
These aren't the most
complicated queries

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:20.100
in the world, but it is
reading a substantial portion

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:24.790
of the data from Cloud
Storage using Presto.

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:27.010
Ultimately, and I've
run this demo enough

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:30.700
and run the costs on all
these different clouds.

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:33.192
We didn't have this cluster
for longer than 10 minutes.

00:30:33.192 --> 00:30:35.650
So we're going to actually be
charged the 10 minute minimum

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:36.910
for Cloud Dataproc.

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:40.090
Everything beyond 10
minutes is per minute.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:45.550
That 210 node cluster
costs $12.85 to run.

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:49.840
If you ran it in Amazon
EMR or HDInsight,

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:53.590
you'd be paying substantially
more respectively, just

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:57.370
based on either our rounding
or just more expensive costs

00:30:57.370 --> 00:30:58.940
on a per minute basis overall.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:06.160
So I've told you
Dataproc is awesome.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:09.480
We have some just kind of
random quotes from Twitter.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:11.480
If you don't believe me,
you can search Twitter,

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:13.980
these are definitely there.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:16.980
We've had both customers
offload from other clouds

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:21.960
to Dataproc or from on
premise to Dataproc.

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:25.050
And we've really tried to
focus on customer feedback

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:26.820
to make Dataproc better.

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:31.920
That's a core part of how we
improve the platform overall.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.770
Ultimately, we also try to share
best practices with customers

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.420
to make using Dataproc easier.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.462
And I want to share some
of those tips with you.

00:31:38.462 --> 00:31:40.920
So if you are interested in
using Spark and Hadoop on Cloud

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:41.490
Platform.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:44.100
Or you are using Cloud
Dataproc already,

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:46.110
you have a better experience.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:49.260
The first tip that we
recommend to a lot of customers

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:52.110
is to split clusters and jobs.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:55.930
Canonically on premise, you most
often will have one cluster,

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:58.350
and everybody will just
submit all of their jobs

00:31:58.350 --> 00:31:59.110
to that cluster.

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:00.840
And they may try and
do it intelligently

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:03.960
and submit their jobs over
time or schedule them,

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:07.620
or they may just submit all of
the jobs and see what happens.

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:09.840
With Cloud Dataproc, you
can have multiple clusters.

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.400
And the clusters
can be right sized

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:14.190
and shaped and
conditioned and configured

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.770
to run specific types of jobs.

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:21.472
So if I have a bunch of jobs
and my first two jobs run pretty

00:32:21.472 --> 00:32:23.430
quickly, and I know I
can run them in parallel,

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:24.900
I can send them
off to Cluster A.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:27.780
If I have two slower jobs
that are very intensive

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:29.490
and I want them
to run separately,

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:31.890
you can run them on
separate clusters.

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:35.640
We also support the use of cloud
labels with Cloud Dataproc.

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:39.180
Labels are a key value
pair that you can

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:41.607
associate with cloud resources.

00:32:41.607 --> 00:32:43.440
With Cloud Dataproc
that's really important,

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.440
because if I have these
three different clusters

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.370
and I wanted to tag
maybe organization

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:51.000
equals A, B, and C, I can then
do cost accounting and billing

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:53.250
accounting based on
those labels and actually

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:55.650
filter and list by
those labels separately.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:58.060
So if you are running Spark
and Hadoop in an enterprise

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:00.060
setting, and you actually
wanted to do something

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:02.910
like be able to figure out
how much people were using

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:05.940
or trace that back,
labels make it very easy.

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:08.070
And splitting clusters and
jobs makes it very easy.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:10.740
And labels can apply to
both Cloud Dataproc clusters

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:12.690
and jobs.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.580
Development and production.

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.675
If you need to have a production
and development environment,

00:33:17.675 --> 00:33:20.190
and you want to have maybe a
staging environment, since you

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:22.110
can have multiple
clusters, it's very easy

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:25.710
to have separate prod,
dev, tests, whatever,

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:26.910
experimentation clusters.

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:28.159
You just create a new cluster.

00:33:28.159 --> 00:33:30.349
The nice thing here is
if, for example, you

00:33:30.349 --> 00:33:31.890
wanted to do some
development and you

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:36.270
didn't want to break or impact
other users or other jobs,

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:38.280
just create a
development cluster.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.130
Additionally, since you can
read data, for instance,

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:43.879
from Cloud Storage for
multiple different clusters,

00:33:43.879 --> 00:33:46.170
if I wanted to have all of
my production work occurring

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.628
and then do some development,
maybe I have a bunch of Spark

00:33:48.628 --> 00:33:50.520
and I want to test
it in Spark 2.2.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:53.040
You can create a new
cluster, use a preview image,

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:55.920
and start testing against
that Cloud Storage bucket.

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:57.720
You don't have to do
anything differently,

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.803
you don't have to copy
data, it's very easy to do.

00:33:59.803 --> 00:34:01.530
And we've had a lot
of success trying

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:03.270
to help customers
migrate to the idea

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:05.540
that you can create
clusters when you need them.

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:08.020
If you don't need them,
you can delete them.

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:09.810
There's really no
reason, honestly, to--

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:12.226
I would advocate if you're not
using a cluster, delete it.

00:34:12.226 --> 00:34:14.310
We don't want you
to keep it around.

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:17.100
That's not good for
you, and ultimately that

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:20.980
just makes it not good for us.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:22.882
So create and delete
clusters often.

00:34:22.882 --> 00:34:24.340
If you have a bunch
of work and you

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:28.270
want to schedule creating and
deleting clusters, you can.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:31.510
As an example, if you are using
something like Apache Airflow,

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:34.179
you can actually create
Cloud Dataproc clusters,

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:36.820
run your jobs using
Apache Airflow,

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:38.560
and then delete your cluster.

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:40.155
Great example of
an ephemeral use

00:34:40.155 --> 00:34:42.280
case where you can create
and delete clusters often

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.099
just based on whenever
you actually need them.

00:34:45.099 --> 00:34:46.909
Cloud Storage.

00:34:46.909 --> 00:34:51.889
A lot of people that shift from
on-premise to Cloud Dataproc

00:34:51.889 --> 00:34:55.429
ask us, what's the
performance of Cloud Storage?

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:56.810
How does it compare with HDFS?

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:58.370
Why should I use Cloud Storage?

00:34:58.370 --> 00:34:59.750
Generally speaking,
Cloud Storage

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:02.120
is going to give you a
very high throughput.

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:03.920
It has its own sets
of features which

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:05.840
you may find really
useful, like being

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:08.540
able to apply security
controls or having auditing

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:10.100
on Cloud Storage buckets.

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:12.100
It also allows you to use
data and Cloud Storage

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:13.974
between many different
Google Cloud products.

00:35:13.974 --> 00:35:15.846
You could do federated
queries with BigQuery

00:35:15.846 --> 00:35:17.360
on that Cloud Storage bucket.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.370
So moving away from
HDFS not only brings

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:22.670
performance and cost benefits
for your Spark and Hadoop use

00:35:22.670 --> 00:35:25.520
cases, but it may provide
a lot of other benefits

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:27.230
for data that would
normally have just

00:35:27.230 --> 00:35:30.850
been living in HDFS, because
that's where it would live.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:32.380
The job submission API.

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:34.450
If you are submitting
work to your clusters,

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:36.629
you can submit work really
in many different ways.

00:35:36.629 --> 00:35:38.420
There's probably an
infinite number of ways

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:39.878
when you look at
it to submit work.

00:35:39.878 --> 00:35:42.400
You could SSH in and
use spark-submit.

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:44.260
You could use
something like Airflow.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:47.290
You could write a Python script
that's sitting on a crontab,

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:50.800
just submitting work whenever
you have it scheduled.

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:53.757
Generally we advocate that you
use our job submission API,

00:35:53.757 --> 00:35:55.340
and there's a lot
of reasons for that.

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:57.173
It allows you to do
things like apply labels

00:35:57.173 --> 00:35:58.450
to jobs, as you saw.

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:01.090
You can see the
output from jobs.

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:03.059
It makes it much
easier, so you only

00:36:03.059 --> 00:36:05.350
have to fill in a few arguments
to actually submit work

00:36:05.350 --> 00:36:05.940
to a cluster.

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:07.330
So with the job
API, we've really

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:09.820
tried to make it easy
for you to broker work

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:15.530
and interact with work and
manage work with clusters.

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:18.620
So as I've sort of shown
you and talked about,

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:21.200
you can scale
clusters at any time.

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:23.450
We scaled the
cluster in the demo.

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:24.890
As I mentioned,
I didn't demo it,

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:29.000
but you can use custom VM types
for your master and worker

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:30.160
nodes.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:32.630
And you can use preemptible
nodes with your clusters

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:34.610
if you want to use
preemptible nodes.

00:36:37.870 --> 00:36:39.790
One thing that I ran
to install Presto

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:41.920
is what I called
initialization action.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:43.840
Initialization
actions are scripts

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:45.620
which can be interpreted
on the cluster,

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:48.203
so these could be shell scripts,
they could be Python scripts,

00:36:48.203 --> 00:36:49.570
they could be Ruby scripts.

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:52.967
These scripts are run after the
services start on the cluster.

00:36:52.967 --> 00:36:54.550
They're really useful
for doing things

00:36:54.550 --> 00:36:57.470
like staging jars or
copying data locally

00:36:57.470 --> 00:36:59.740
that you might want to
test on or in this case

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:02.470
as we saw, installing Presto.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:05.380
We also have a mechanism
through our client tooling

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:07.970
to allow you to set
properties in cluster files.

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:11.070
So if you wanted to modify, say,
properties in the core site XML

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:13.740
file, we have a way for
you to easily do that.

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:16.060
So you don't have to go
in and SSH in or write

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:17.920
an initialization
action to do it.

00:37:17.920 --> 00:37:20.830
So we've tried to create two
really powerful but easy ways

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:26.460
for you to customize clusters
when you start them up.

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.750
There is a bunch of new
Cloud Dataproc features

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:30.520
that I want to talk
about that we've

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:33.940
released in the last few weeks
in anticipation for next.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:35.470
We've actually
gotten a few more out

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:37.970
that I can talk about as well.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.540
The first is restartable jobs.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:43.090
So in our API and
our client tooling,

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:47.560
traditionally when a job ran
and failed, that was sort of it.

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:50.020
The job would just
sit and have failed.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:51.880
In some cases, this
may not be desirable.

00:37:51.880 --> 00:37:55.030
You actually may want
a job to restart.

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:56.920
For example, you
may have a streaming

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:58.510
job that just runs
out of memory,

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:00.700
and it was kind
of a corner case.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:02.462
And you want that
job to restart.

00:38:02.462 --> 00:38:04.920
With our client tooling now,
when you submit a job to Cloud

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:06.700
Dataproc, you can
actually specify

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.010
the number of times
per hour that you

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:10.690
want that job to restart.

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:14.020
This is really useful
for long-running jobs.

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:17.200
We designed it with kind of an
eye on streaming applications,

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:19.690
as we looked to better
support and provide

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:22.720
support for streaming
applications on Dataproc.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.120
So that way you can be confident
that if your work fails,

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:29.150
we're going to kick
off that job for you.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:32.060
GPUs were recently
announced on Cloud Platform

00:38:32.060 --> 00:38:34.070
and made available
to Compute Engine.

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:36.050
This is a good example
of when innovations

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:38.750
occur on other parts
of the Cloud Platform.

00:38:38.750 --> 00:38:41.360
If they make sense to bring to
the Spark and Hadoop ecosystem,

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:43.392
we absolutely want to.

00:38:43.392 --> 00:38:45.350
So when you create Cloud
Dataproc clusters now,

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:48.410
you can specify the
number of accelerators,

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:50.990
which are essentially
GPUs, that you want attach

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:53.150
to either your master
or worker nodes

00:38:53.150 --> 00:38:59.160
in your cluster from zero,
which is the default, up to 8.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.700
If anybody is really
interested in GPUs and Spark,

00:39:01.700 --> 00:39:05.000
I think you would probably know
this is an evolving ecosystem,

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:09.800
and I think the Spark support
and kind of other ecosystem

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:12.057
support for GPUs is
an evolving story.

00:39:12.057 --> 00:39:13.640
But there's a lot
of cases we actually

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:15.420
may want to use GPUs
now or in the future,

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:17.086
and it was very
important to us to allow

00:39:17.086 --> 00:39:22.220
you to use them with Cloud
Dataproc as soon as possible.

00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:25.220
I'd briefly mentioned
single node clusters.

00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:27.890
A common request that
we had from customers

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:30.456
was they wanted to just
run very small tests,

00:39:30.456 --> 00:39:32.580
and they actually didn't
need a full blown cluster.

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.460
So they just wanted to run a
really quick test using Spark.

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:38.020
They wanted to do really
lightweight data science.

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:40.130
With single node
clusters, you create

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:41.960
a cluster which
is just one node,

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:44.000
it's the master and worker node.

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.280
That way you don't have to
create a full blown Spark

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:48.740
and Hadoop cluster,
because for something

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:51.890
like, if you're leading
a class, for example,

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:56.060
on how to use Spark, you don't
need giant clusters to do that.

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:58.880
You actually just need
probably a smallish VM

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:00.010
that has Spark installed.

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:01.510
And with single
node clusters, we've

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:03.440
really tried to
make it easy for you

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:06.240
to create these lightweight
clusters for experimentation

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:09.990
and development.

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:12.780
Regional endpoints and
private IP addresses.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.020
So with Cloud Dataproc, when
we launched almost a year ago

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:19.890
our API and the client
tools that used our API,

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:23.070
used one region,
which was global.

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:28.110
Recently we've added individual
regions for each Compute Engine

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:29.370
region and Cloud Dataproc.

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:32.730
So in our API and
via our client tools,

00:40:32.730 --> 00:40:34.950
you can now isolate
to a specific region.

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:38.490
So you could say, if
you're using our tools,

00:40:38.490 --> 00:40:41.220
say use the US region.

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:43.740
That means the calls are
going to that region.

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:46.230
We've distributed
our control plane,

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:48.810
and essentially we've
stood up individual stacks

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:51.630
of the control infrastructure
for Cloud Dataproc and all

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:53.400
of these individual regions.

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:56.320
The benefit there is you
may get better performance.

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:59.010
So for example, if
you are in Europe

00:40:59.010 --> 00:41:00.900
and you wanted to use
the Europe region,

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:02.640
you may get much
better performance

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:06.240
by isolating to that region
for applications that

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:09.540
are working entirely in Europe.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.150
Clusters and cloud
resources have--

00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:13.635
going to private IPs.

00:41:13.635 --> 00:41:15.240
Clusters and
resources have often

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:18.000
had public IPs attached to them.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:21.060
One very, very common request
from large enterprise customers

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.100
especially was, I
want clusters but I

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:25.710
don't want them to have a
public IP attached, because that

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:28.840
makes my security
people very sadface.

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:33.120
So with the recent launch
of Cloudpath last week,

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:36.600
we now support creating
clusters that do not have

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:39.060
a public IP attached to them.

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:40.560
And an important
differentiator here

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:42.070
is we try to make it very easy.

00:41:42.070 --> 00:41:44.760
So this isn't something
that's unique to Dataproc.

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:47.010
But the way that we've tried
to innovate and make life

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:48.990
better for all Google
Cloud customers here

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:52.200
is to make it very easy to
create clusters that don't have

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:55.170
a public IP attached to them.

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:57.270
Both of these
features are in beta,

00:41:57.270 --> 00:42:01.725
and they were both commonly
requested features.

00:42:01.725 --> 00:42:04.465
And that drives back
to, we try to respond

00:42:04.465 --> 00:42:05.340
to customer feedback.

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:08.010
So honestly, if you are
interested in using Dataproc,

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:11.220
or you are and you have
ideas, requests, gripes,

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:12.900
loves, complaints,
send them our way.

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:16.890
We try to be very, very
customer-focused and hands on.

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:18.750
How to get started
with Cloud Dataproc.

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.030
There's a few different
sessions which

00:42:21.030 --> 00:42:24.780
either directly or indirectly
mention Cloud Dataproc.

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.540
Some of these sessions have
unfortunately already passed.

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:31.300
But the good news is, I believe
the videos will be on YouTube.

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:35.670
So if you are interested,
this is really

00:42:35.670 --> 00:42:38.490
kind of the introduction
to a lot of these concepts.

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:41.310
A lot of these other tools
go into very specific ideas

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:44.010
and concepts.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:46.260
If you want to get hands-on
literally right now,

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:49.410
there is a ton of
Codelabs downstairs.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:52.410
You can get started with
Cloud Dataproc Codelabs.

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:54.900
We also have a set of
quickstarts and tutorials

00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.900
to show you how to create
clusters and submit jobs.

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:01.080
And just to sort of go back
to the initialization actions,

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:03.510
a lot of what we do
that's customer-facing,

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:07.630
that's open source, we have in
various GitHub repositories.

00:43:07.630 --> 00:43:09.520
So we have our
initialization actions,

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:13.640
for example, for installing
Zeppelin and Presto there.

00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:15.990
If you need help, there's
many different ways

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:17.960
you can get help
with Cloud Dataproc.

00:43:17.960 --> 00:43:20.330
We have documentation online.

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:22.910
We also have our Release Notes.

00:43:22.910 --> 00:43:26.570
I again have to give the
engineering team credit here.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:28.520
We release updates
to Cloud Dataproc

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:31.354
fairly frequently, which
we really take pride in

00:43:31.354 --> 00:43:33.020
because we're trying
to bring the latest

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:35.120
and greatest of
this large ecosystem

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:37.710
with a ton of innovations
to make it better.

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:39.975
So if you have been or are
interested in Dataproc,

00:43:39.975 --> 00:43:41.600
watch our Release
Notes pretty closely,

00:43:41.600 --> 00:43:43.641
because they usually will
change about every week

00:43:43.641 --> 00:43:45.140
for the better.

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:47.390
If you actually
want hands-on help,

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:50.380
a really good place is Stack
Overflow, using the Google

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:52.190
Cloud Dataproc tag.

00:43:52.190 --> 00:43:55.370
You could probably
indirectly meet many members

00:43:55.370 --> 00:43:58.280
of the Cloud Dataproc
team based on just answers

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:01.400
from Stack Overflow.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:04.640
And then we have our
informal email list.

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:07.160
And if you are a Google
Cloud support customer,

00:44:07.160 --> 00:44:09.930
that's also an option as well.

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:11.672
And with that, if
there's any questions,

00:44:11.672 --> 00:44:12.630
I'm happy to take them.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:14.990
Other than that, thank
you guys very much.

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:18.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

